6 Design Patterns
The architecture can be understood as a composition of several classical software design patterns. The strategy pattern governs the selection of execution modes, where different task kinds correspond to different execution strategies. The factory pattern centralizes task creation, ensuring consistent construction of runtime objects. The builder pattern appears in the incremental assembly of execution parameters, including worker type, prompt, and permissions. The state machine pattern captures lifecycle transitions such as pending, running, and completed states. Finally, the registry pattern provides persistent storage and indexing for tasks. Together, these patterns create a coherent and extensible runtime structure.
The following skeleton makes the separation of responsibilities explicit.

6.1 Routing
from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import Optional


@dataclass
class DelegationRequest:
    description: str
    prompt: str
    team_name: Optional[str] = None
    worker_name: Optional[str] = None
    isolation: str = "local"
    run_in_background: bool = False


class TaskRouter:
    def route(self, request: DelegationRequest) -&gt; TaskKind:
        if request.team_name and request.worker_name:
            return TaskKind.TEAMMATE

        if request.isolation == "remote":
            return TaskKind.REMOTE

        if request.run_in_background:
            return TaskKind.LOCAL_BACKGROUND

        return TaskKind.LOCAL_FOREGROUND


6.2 Factory
class TaskFactory:
    def create(self, request: DelegationRequest) -&gt; Task:
        kind = TaskRouter().route(request)
        return create_task(
            kind=kind,
            description=request.description,
            prompt=request.prompt,
        )


6.3 Registry
class TaskRegistry:
    def __init__(self) -&gt; None:
        self._tasks: dict[str, Task] = {}

    def add(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        self._tasks[task.id] = task

    def get(self, task_id: str) -&gt; Optional[Task]:
        return self._tasks.get(task_id)

    def update_status(self, task_id: str, status: TaskStatus) -&gt; None:
        task = self._tasks[task_id]
        task.status = status
        if status in {TaskStatus.COMPLETED, TaskStatus.FAILED, TaskStatus.KILLED}:
            task.end_time = time.time()

    def all(self) -&gt; list[Task]:
        return list(self._tasks.values())


6.4 Runner
class TaskRunner:
    def run(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        task.status = TaskStatus.RUNNING

        try:
            if task.kind == TaskKind.REMOTE:
                self._run_remote(task)
            elif task.kind == TaskKind.TEAMMATE:
                self._run_teammate(task)
            elif task.kind == TaskKind.LOCAL_BACKGROUND:
                self._run_background(task)
            else:
                self._run_foreground(task)

            task.status = TaskStatus.COMPLETED
            task.end_time = time.time()
        except Exception:
            task.status = TaskStatus.FAILED
            task.end_time = time.time()
            raise

    def _run_remote(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        pass

    def _run_teammate(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        pass

    def _run_background(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        pass

    def _run_foreground(self, task: Task) -&gt; None:
        pass


6.5 Runtime
class AgentRuntime:
    def __init__(self) -&gt; None:
        self.factory = TaskFactory()
        self.registry = TaskRegistry()
        self.runner = TaskRunner()

    def delegate(self, request: DelegationRequest) -&gt; Task:
        task = self.factory.create(request)
        self.registry.add(task)
        self.runner.run(task)
        return task
This structure enforces a clear decomposition. The policy layer governs intent. The routing layer selects execution. The factory constructs the task contract. The registry maintains state. The runner executes the task.
Many system failures arise from architectural issues rather than model limitations. If the model directly controls execution semantics, the system becomes inconsistent because prompts do not provide stable guarantees. If background work is not represented explicitly, the system loses observability and control. If routing logic is distributed across components, behavior becomes fragmented. If policy and runtime are tightly coupled, small prompt changes can produce unintended execution differences. These failure modes are avoided by maintaining strict abstraction boundaries.
A concise summary of the architecture is given by the following principle.

Let the model choose intent. Let code choose execution semantics.

This separation ensures that reasoning and execution remain independently evolvable. The policy can improve decision quality, while the runtime preserves consistency and reliability. The central problem in agent systems is not merely how to expose tools to a model. A more fundamental problem is how to transform a high-level decision into a reliable runtime object. Routing serves as this transformation layer. It connects model intent to execution reality. In any non-trivial coordinator-worker system, this layer is not auxiliary infrastructure. It is the core of the agent builder.

3 The Agentic Loop as a Managed Execution Lifecycle
OpenClaw defines the agentic loop as the full authoritative execution path of a run: intake, context assembly, model inference, tool execution, streaming replies, and persistence. Runs are serialized per session key, optionally through a global lane, to prevent tool and session races while keeping history consistent.
export async function runEmbeddedPiAgent(
  params: RunEmbeddedPiAgentParams,
): Promise&lt;EmbeddedPiRunResult&gt; {
  const sessionLane = resolveSessionLane(params.sessionKey?.trim() || params.sessionId);
  const globalLane = resolveGlobalLane(params.lane);

  const enqueueGlobal =
    params.enqueue ?? ((task, opts) =&gt; enqueueCommandInLane(globalLane, task, opts));
  const enqueueSession =
    params.enqueue ?? ((task, opts) =&gt; enqueueCommandInLane(sessionLane, task, opts));

  return enqueueSession(() =&gt;
    enqueueGlobal(async () =&gt; {
      // run body
    }),
  );
}
In practice, this means a turn is treated as an execution unit with scheduling, locking, streaming, and usage metadata—not as a bare completion call. The runtime validates the request, resolves the session, persists metadata, loads the skills snapshot, subscribes to agent events, enforces timeouts, and returns usage metadata at the end of the run.

3.1 Perception
When a new message arrives, OpenClaw does not always treat it as a brand-new prompt append. The queueing layer first determines how that message should enter the system: it can steer the current run, wait for a follow-up turn, or be collected with other queued messages. In steer mode, inbound input can be injected into the active run and pending tool calls are canceled after the next tool boundary. That makes the message part of a live control flow, not just another line of chat history.


3.2 Reasoning
Once the system commits to a turn, it follows a dedicated lifecycle. Workspace resolution, bootstrap-context injection, skills loading or reuse from snapshot, session locking, prompt assembly, model execution, and event bridging are all part of the run path.
const started = Date.now();

const workspaceResolution = resolveRunWorkspaceDir({
  workspaceDir: params.workspaceDir,
  sessionKey: params.sessionKey,
  agentId: params.agentId,
  config: params.config,
});

const resolvedWorkspace = workspaceResolution.workspaceDir;

const hookRunner = getGlobalHookRunner();
const hookCtx = {
  agentId: workspaceResolution.agentId,
  sessionKey: params.sessionKey,
  sessionId: params.sessionId,
  workspaceDir: resolvedWorkspace,
  messageProvider: params.messageProvider ?? undefined,
  trigger: params.trigger,
  channelId: params.messageChannel ?? params.messageProvider ?? undefined,
};

if (hookRunner?.hasHooks("before_model_resolve")) {
  modelResolveOverride = await hookRunner.runBeforeModelResolve(
    { prompt: params.prompt },
    hookCtx,
  );
}

const { model, error, authStorage, modelRegistry } = resolveModel(
  provider,
  modelId,
  agentDir,
  params.config,
);
OpenClaw therefore treats a run as a managed lifecycle with consistency guarantees, not simply as an isolated model call.


3.3 Action
Tool execution is a first-class element of that loop. OpenClaw bridges tool events into the agent stream, distinguishes assistant deltas from tool deltas and lifecycle events, and supports block streaming with tool-start summaries and Control UI streaming of tool output when available. The result is a clearer separation between assistant output and tool activity than in systems where everything is flattened into one text stream.
const subscription = subscribeEmbeddedPiSession({
  session: activeSession,
  runId: params.runId,
  onToolResult: params.onToolResult,
  onReasoningStream: params.onReasoningStream,
  onBlockReply: params.onBlockReply,
  onPartialReply: params.onPartialReply,
  onAgentEvent: params.onAgentEvent,
});


3.4 Feedback
OpenClaw also treats observability as part of the run. Its logging and diagnostics cover queueing behavior, session state, run timing, token usage, cost, and message-processing events. This does not make the system magically infallible, but it does mean the runtime is instrumented in a way that supports operation and debugging rather than leaving behavior opaque.
Ultimately, OpenClaw’s main difference is not that it has tools, but that it treats the agent loop as a managed, observable, and serialized runtime. Input can steer an ongoing run, tool activity is surfaced as structured events, and execution is organized with queueing and accounting discipline rather than left to an implicit chat loop.
This is the difference between an agent that merely calls tools and an agent that can be operated. Once runs become long-lived and interactive, scheduling, serialization, and event boundaries are not implementation details; they are core product behavior.
